<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#f2f5f1">
  <title>CISA Study Desk | Flashcards</title>
  <style>
    :root {
      color-scheme: light;
      --paper: #f2f5f1;
      --surface: #ffffff;
      --ink: #172521;
      --muted: #64736e;
      --line: #d8e0da;
      --green: #1d6652;
      --green-dark: #124938;
      --green-wash: #e4f1eb;
      --coral: #b74936;
      --coral-wash: #fff0ec;
      --gold: #a66a13;
      --gold-wash: #fff5dc;
      --shadow: 0 24px 70px rgba(23, 37, 33, .09);
    }

    * { box-sizing: border-box; }

    body {
      min-height: 100vh;
      margin: 0;
      color: var(--ink);
      background:
        radial-gradient(ellipse at 88% 12%, rgba(201, 224, 212, .62), transparent 31rem),
        linear-gradient(135deg, #f4f6f3 0%, var(--paper) 50%, #edf2ee 100%);
      font-family: "Avenir Next", Avenir, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
      line-height: 1.55;
    }

    button, input { font: inherit; }

    .topbar {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: 1rem;
      max-width: 1120px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 1.25rem 2rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(23, 37, 33, .12);
    }

    .brand {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: .7rem;
      font-family: "Iowan Old Style", "Palatino Linotype", Georgia, serif;
      font-size: 1.2rem;
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .brand-mark {
      display: grid;
      width: 2rem;
      aspect-ratio: 1;
      place-items: center;
      color: #fff;
      background: var(--green);
      border-radius: 6px;
      font-family: "Avenir Next", Avenir, sans-serif;
      font-size: .75rem;
      letter-spacing: .04em;
    }

    .save-status {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: .5rem;
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: .8rem;
    }

    .save-dot {
      width: .5rem;
      aspect-ratio: 1;
      background: #4b9277;
      border-radius: 50%;
    }

    main {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 250px;
      align-items: start;
      gap: 2rem;
      max-width: 1120px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 3.5rem 2rem 5rem;
      animation: arrive .45s ease-out both;
    }

    @keyframes arrive {
      from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(8px); }
      to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
    }

    .eyebrow {
      margin: 0 0 .55rem;
      color: var(--green);
      font-size: .72rem;
      font-weight: 750;
      letter-spacing: .1em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    h1, h2, p { margin-top: 0; }

    h1 {
      margin-bottom: 1.8rem;
      font-family: "Iowan Old Style", "Palatino Linotype", Georgia, serif;
      font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.55rem);
      font-weight: 600;
      line-height: 1.14;
    }

    .question-card {
      background: var(--surface);
      border: 1px solid rgba(23, 37, 33, .09);
      border-radius: 8px;
      box-shadow: var(--shadow);
    }

    .question-card { padding: clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 2.25rem); }

    .question-meta {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: .65rem;
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: .76rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      letter-spacing: .07em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    .question-number { color: var(--green); }
    .meta-divider { width: 1px; height: .9rem; background: var(--line); }

    .prompt {
      max-width: 66ch;
      margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
      font-family: "Iowan Old Style", "Palatino Linotype", Georgia, serif;
      font-size: 1.18rem;
      line-height: 1.55;
    }

    fieldset { display: grid; gap: .65rem; margin: 0; padding: 0; border: 0; }
    legend { margin-bottom: .75rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: .84rem; }

    .choice {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1.65rem 1fr;
      align-items: start;
      gap: .9rem;
      min-height: 3.7rem;
      padding: .9rem 1rem;
      background: #fbfcfb;
      border: 1px solid var(--line);
      border-radius: 6px;
      cursor: pointer;
      transition: border-color .15s ease, background-color .15s ease, transform .15s ease;
    }

    .choice:hover { border-color: #8ab2a2; transform: translateY(-1px); }
    .choice:has(input:checked) { background: var(--green-wash); border-color: var(--green); }
    .choice:has(input:focus-visible) { outline: 3px solid rgba(29, 102, 82, .25); outline-offset: 2px; }

    .choice input {
      width: 1.1rem;
      height: 1.1rem;
      margin: .12rem 0 0;
      accent-color: var(--green);
    }

    .choice-key { margin-right: .25rem; color: var(--green-dark); font-weight: 750; }
    .choice-text { font-size: .94rem; }

    .actions {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: 1rem;
      margin-top: 1.25rem;
    }

    .attempt-count { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: .82rem; }

    .side-rail { padding-top: .35rem; }
    .progress-label { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; color: var(--muted); font-size: .78rem; }
    .progress-track { height: 5px; margin: .6rem 0 1.6rem; overflow: hidden; background: #dce4de; border-radius: 99px; }
    .progress-fill { width: 0; height: 100%; background: var(--green); transition: width .25s ease; }

    .side-note {
      padding: 1rem 0 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .side-note h2 { margin-bottom: .45rem; font-size: .88rem; }
    .side-note p { margin-bottom: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: .82rem; }
    .source-note { margin-top: 1rem !important; font-size: .73rem !important; }
    .noscript-note { max-width: 1120px; margin: 0 auto; padding: .7rem 2rem; color: #70480d; background: var(--gold-wash); font-size: .85rem; }

    .flashcard-toolbar { display: flex; align-items: end; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .8rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
    .filter-control { display: grid; gap: .25rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: .74rem; font-weight: 700; }
    .filter-control select { min-height: 2.5rem; padding: .4rem 2rem .4rem .6rem; color: var(--ink); background: white; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 5px; }
    .shuffle-button, .flash-nav-button, .reveal-button, .mark-button { min-height: 2.55rem; padding: .5rem .8rem; color: var(--green-dark); background: var(--green-wash); border: 0; border-radius: 5px; cursor: pointer; font-size: .83rem; font-weight: 700; }
    .shuffle-button:hover, .flash-nav-button:hover, .reveal-button:hover, .mark-button:hover { background: #d2e8de; }
    .flash-nav-button:disabled { opacity: .5; cursor: not-allowed; }
    .flashcard-question { max-width: 60ch; margin: 0 0 1.35rem; font-family: "Iowan Old Style", "Palatino Linotype", Georgia, serif; font-size: 1.45rem; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.4; }
    .flashcard-answer { margin: 0 0 1.1rem; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; background: #f3f7f4; border-left: 3px solid var(--green); border-radius: 0 5px 5px 0; }
    .flashcard-answer[hidden], .flashcard-empty[hidden] { display: none; }
    .flashcard-answer p { margin: .25rem 0 0; }
    .flashcard-source { margin: 1rem 0 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: .75rem; }
    .flashcard-actions { display: flex; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .6rem; margin-top: 1rem; }
    .flashcard-actions .spacer { flex: 1; }
    .mark-button.is-marked { color: white; background: var(--green); }
    .flashcard-status { min-height: 1.3rem; margin: .7rem 0 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: .8rem; }
    .flashcard-empty { padding: 1rem; color: var(--muted); background: white; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px; }

    @media (max-width: 760px) {
      .topbar { flex-wrap: wrap; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; }
      main { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1.5rem; padding: 2.25rem 1.1rem 3rem; }
      .side-rail { order: -1; }
      .progress-track { margin-bottom: .75rem; }
      .side-note { display: none; }
    }

    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      *, *::before, *::after { scroll-behavior: auto !important; animation-duration: .01ms !important; transition-duration: .01ms !important; }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header class="topbar">
    <div class="brand"><span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true">C</span><span>CISA Study Desk</span></div>
    <div class="save-status"><span class="save-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Progress saved on this device</span></div>
  </header>
  <noscript><p class="noscript-note">This file preview is not running the flashcard controls. Open this page in Safari or another web browser to navigate the deck.</p></noscript>

  <main>
    <section id="flashcard-view" aria-labelledby="flashcard-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">Flashcards / Active recall</p>
      <h1 id="flashcard-title">Recall first.<br>Then reveal.</h1>
      <div class="flashcard-toolbar" aria-label="Flashcard controls">
        <label class="filter-control" for="domain-filter">Domain
          <select id="domain-filter">
            <option value="all">All domains</option>
            <option value="1">1 · IS Auditing Process</option>
            <option value="2">2 · Governance and Management</option>
            <option value="3">3 · Acquisition and Development</option>
            <option value="4">4 · Operations and Resilience</option>
            <option value="5">5 · Protection of Information Assets</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <label class="filter-control" for="status-filter">Study queue
          <select id="status-filter">
            <option value="all">All cards</option>
            <option value="review">Needs review</option>
            <option value="mastered">Mastered</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <button class="shuffle-button" id="shuffle-cards" type="button">Shuffle cards</button>
      </div>
      <article class="question-card" aria-labelledby="flashcard-prompt">
        <div class="question-meta">
          <span class="question-number" id="flashcard-domain">Domain 1 · 1A</span>
          <span class="meta-divider" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span id="flashcard-count">Card 1 of 248</span>
        </div>
        <h2 class="flashcard-question" id="flashcard-prompt">What is the purpose of risk-based audit planning?</h2>
        <button class="reveal-button" id="reveal-answer" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="flashcard-answer">Show answer</button>
        <div class="flashcard-answer" id="flashcard-answer" hidden>
          <strong>Answer</strong>
          <p id="flashcard-answer-text">Direct audit resources toward the areas with greatest risk and align coverage to business objectives.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="flashcard-source" id="flashcard-source">Source: Domain 1A handout · CISA Official Review Manual (2024), Chapter 1</p>
        <div class="flashcard-actions">
          <button class="flash-nav-button" id="previous-card" type="button">Previous</button>
          <button class="mark-button" id="mark-review" type="button">Need review</button>
          <button class="mark-button" id="mark-mastered" type="button">Got it</button>
          <span class="spacer" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <button class="flash-nav-button" id="next-card" type="button">Next</button>
        </div>
        <p class="flashcard-status" id="flashcard-status" aria-live="polite"></p>
      </article>
      <p class="flashcard-empty" id="flashcard-empty" hidden>No cards match this queue yet. Choose another queue or mark cards as you study.</p>
      <p class="source-note">Condensed original prompts based on topics in the supplied CISA domain handouts and CISA Official Review Manual (2024). They are not exhaustive coverage or reproduced exam questions; use the manuals and QAE practice for deeper review.</p>
    </section>

    <aside class="side-rail" aria-label="Session progress">
      <div class="progress-label"><span>Session progress</span><span id="progress-text">0 / 248 mastered</span></div>
      <div class="progress-track" aria-hidden="true"><div class="progress-fill" id="progress-fill"></div></div>
      <div class="side-note">
        <h2>Deck focus</h2>
        <p>Concept review across all five CISA exam domains.</p>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </main>

  <script>
    const FLASHCARD_STORAGE_KEY = "cisa-study-desk-flashcards-v1";
    const flashcards = [
      [1,"1A","What is the purpose of risk-based audit planning?","Direct audit resources toward the areas with greatest risk and align coverage to business objectives."],
      [1,"1A","How do preventive, detective, and corrective controls differ?","Preventive controls aim to stop an event; detective controls identify it; corrective controls remediate its effects."],
      [1,"1A","What should an auditor establish before relying on a control?","Understand its design and implementation, then test operating effectiveness over the relevant period."],
      [1,"1A","What is the difference between inherent and residual risk?","Inherent risk exists before controls. Residual risk remains after controls and risk responses are applied."],
      [1,"1A","What makes audit evidence persuasive?","It should be sufficient in quantity and relevant and reliable in quality. Independently obtained evidence is generally stronger than unsupported inquiry."],
      [1,"1A","Why preserve auditor independence and objectivity?","They support unbiased conclusions. Auditors should disclose conflicts and avoid operational responsibility for activities they later assess."],
      [1,"1A","What does an audit charter establish?","The audit function's purpose, authority, responsibilities, reporting relationships, and access needed to perform its work."],
      [1,"1A","How does an audit engagement differ from consulting?","An audit provides independent assurance; consulting advises or facilitates. Safeguards ensure consulting does not impair later assurance."],
      [1,"1A","How should an auditor respond when management accepts risk above tolerance?","Escalate it to appropriate management or governance for an informed decision; the auditor does not accept risk for management."],
      [1,"1A","When is a compliance test useful?","When the auditor needs evidence that a control or required procedure operated as prescribed during the period under review."],
      [1,"1B","How do statistical and nonstatistical sampling differ?","Statistical sampling uses probability theory to quantify sampling risk. Nonstatistical sampling relies on auditor judgment without mathematical quantification."],
      [1,"1B","What does a larger sample generally do to sampling risk?","It generally reduces sampling risk, but not nonsampling risk such as using the wrong procedure or misinterpreting evidence."],
      [1,"1B","What is the purpose of audit workpapers?","Document work, evidence, reasoning, and conclusions so another qualified auditor can understand and review the engagement."],
      [1,"1B","How does data analytics complement sampling?","Analytics can examine broad datasets for patterns and anomalies; sampling tests selected items. Data quality and interpretation still need validation."],
      [1,"1B","What belongs in a useful audit finding?","A clear condition and criteria, the cause and effect or risk, and a practical recommendation or agreed corrective action."],
      [1,"1B","Why validate findings with the auditee before reporting?","To confirm accuracy, resolve misunderstandings, and obtain management's response without surrendering the auditor's independent conclusion."],
      [1,"1B","What is the goal of audit follow-up?","Determine whether corrective actions were implemented and adequately address the risk; escalate unresolved high-risk issues."],
      [1,"1B","What should an auditor do when evidence conflicts with management's explanation?","Investigate and obtain corroborating evidence before concluding; do not rely on an explanation alone."],
      [1,"1B","What is the purpose of an exit meeting?","Discuss significant findings, confirm factual accuracy, and understand management responses before finalizing the report."],
      [1,"1B","What should drive the priority of an audit recommendation?","The risk and potential business impact of the finding, not simply how easy the recommendation is to implement."],
      [1,"1B","An auditor finds exceptions in a sample. What should happen before projecting the result to the full population?","Evaluate the cause of exceptions and whether the sample supports a conclusion; expand testing if needed."],
      [1,"1A","A control exists in policy but staff do not follow it. What has the auditor established?","The control may be designed appropriately but is not operating effectively; assess the gap, risk, and cause."],
      [1,"1A","How do general IT controls and application controls differ?","General controls support the IT environment; application controls address processing in specific systems, such as input, processing, and output checks."],
      [1,"1A","What is detection risk in an audit?","The risk that audit procedures fail to detect an existing issue; the auditor manages it through appropriate procedure design and execution."],
      [1,"1A","A system owner rates a sensitive-data system as low risk. What should the auditor do?","Validate the assessment against evidence, classification, threats, and business impact rather than relying on an unsupported assertion."],
      [1,"1B","Why document the sampling rationale and method?","It lets reviewers assess whether the population, selection method, and conclusions support the audit objective."],
      [1,"1B","Management disputes a finding but provides no new evidence. Should the auditor remove it?","No. Consider the response fairly, but retain a supported finding and report unresolved disagreement accurately."],
      [2,"2A","How do IT governance and IT management differ?","Governance evaluates stakeholder needs, sets direction, and monitors outcomes. Management plans and executes activities within that direction."],
      [2,"2A","Who is accountable for enterprise governance of IT?","The board and executive leadership provide governance and oversight; management executes the approved direction."],
      [2,"2A","How do an IT strategy committee and steering committee differ?","A strategy committee advises at board level on long-term direction. A steering committee coordinates management priorities and project oversight."],
      [2,"2A","What are the three lines in the risk-management model?","Operational management owns day-to-day risks; risk and compliance functions support and monitor; internal audit independently assures the first two lines."],
      [2,"2A","Order policy, standard, guideline, and procedure from broadest to most operational.","Policy states intent; standards set mandatory requirements; guidelines recommend practices; procedures provide step-by-step instructions."],
      [2,"2A","What do risk appetite and risk tolerance describe?","Appetite is the broad amount and type of risk the enterprise is willing to pursue or retain. Tolerance defines acceptable variation or specific limits."],
      [2,"2A","What should an auditor assess about regulatory compliance?","Whether obligations are identified, accountability assigned, and processes effectively monitor, enforce, and demonstrate compliance."],
      [2,"2A","What is the purpose of enterprise architecture?","Align business processes, information, applications, and technology so IT capabilities support strategy and change is managed coherently."],
      [2,"2A","Who defines data classification and handling requirements?","Business data owners set classification and protection needs; custodians implement the required safeguards and handling practices."],
      [2,"2A","Why is privacy an enterprise governance responsibility?","Privacy obligations affect organization-wide processes and controls, requiring clear accountability, policies, and oversight."],
      [2,"2B","What should determine whether an IT investment is continued or stopped?","Its business value, strategic alignment, cost, risk, and contribution to the enterprise project portfolio."],
      [2,"2B","Why are required vacations and job rotation useful controls?","They reduce dependence on one person and can expose irregularities hidden when duties are never independently performed."],
      [2,"2B","What access action is essential when an employee leaves or changes roles?","Revoke unneeded access promptly and issue only what the new role requires; recover enterprise assets and credentials as applicable."],
      [2,"2B","How do capital and operating expenses generally differ?","Capital expenses acquire or improve longer-lived assets; operating expenses support recurring services. Classification follows accounting policy and substance."],
      [2,"2B","Why use chargeback or showback for IT services?","It makes IT consumption costs visible to business units and can improve planning and responsible use of shared resources."],
      [2,"2B","What should a service-level agreement make clear?","Services, measurable performance expectations, responsibilities, reporting, and agreed remedies or escalation paths."],
      [2,"2B","What should an auditor assess about IT performance measures?","Whether measures are meaningful, reliable, aligned to objectives, and used to address performance gaps."],
      [2,"2B","Why document succession and cross-training for key IT roles?","To preserve essential services and knowledge when key employees are unavailable or leave, reducing continuity risks."],
      [2,"2B","What is the purpose of an IT business case?","Explain expected benefits, costs, risks, and strategic alignment so decision-makers can compare options and authorize investment."],
      [2,"2B","Why should IT responsibilities and reporting relationships be clear?","Clear accountability supports decisions, segregation of duties, escalation, and oversight of IT risks and services."],
      [2,"2A","A proposed IT project has strong technical benefits but weak business alignment. What is the governance concern?","The investment may not deliver enterprise value; decision-makers should evaluate strategic alignment and opportunity cost before approval."],
      [2,"2A","Who owns enterprise risk, and what is internal audit's role?","Management owns and responds to risk. Internal audit independently assesses whether risk management and controls are effective."],
      [2,"2A","A new privacy regulation applies. What should an auditor first assess?","Whether obligations are identified, accountability assigned, and processes established to implement and monitor compliance."],
      [2,"2B","Why should IT performance measures include business outcomes as well as uptime?","Uptime alone may not show whether IT delivers value or meets user needs; measures should align with service and enterprise objectives."],
      [2,"2B","A vendor hosts a critical service. Does outsourcing transfer management accountability?","No. Management remains accountable and should oversee vendor risk, service performance, security obligations, and continuity arrangements."],
      [2,"2B","What is the purpose of segregation of duties in IT operations?","Prevent one person from controlling conflicting steps, reducing the opportunity for error or fraud to go undetected."],
      [2,"2B","One administrator is the only person who understands a critical system. What is the concern?","Continuity and concentration risk; use documentation, cross-training, succession planning, and controlled backup access."],
      [3,"3A","What is the purpose of a project charter?","Formally authorize a project, set high-level objectives and scope, identify key roles, and give the project manager a mandate."],
      [3,"3A","How do project governance and project management differ?","Governance provides oversight and strategic alignment; project management delivers approved work within scope, schedule, and budget."],
      [3,"3A","Who funds a project, who governs it, and who manages daily delivery?","The sponsor champions and funds it; the steering committee oversees it; the project manager coordinates daily delivery."],
      [3,"3A","What is the project triple constraint?","Scope, time, and cost constrain one another; changing one may affect the others and the outcome's quality."],
      [3,"3A","How does a matrix project structure affect authority?","Authority is shared by the project manager and functional managers, so responsibilities and escalation paths must be clear."],
      [3,"3A","Why assess feasibility before committing to a solution?","Determine whether it is viable from business, technical, economic, operational, and schedule perspectives before major investment."],
      [3,"3A","What should an auditor look for at project stage gates?","Required deliverables, risks, approvals, and quality criteria reviewed before the project advances or commits further."],
      [3,"3A","What is the purpose of a postimplementation review?","Compare results with objectives and requirements, identify control or operational issues, and capture lessons after deployment."],
      [3,"3A","What is a key control concern when acquiring a vendor system?","Address requirements, security, service expectations, ownership, and exit or continuity provisions through due diligence and agreements."],
      [3,"3A","Why establish clear project roles and responsibilities?","Ensure decisions, deliverables, risk ownership, and approvals are assigned and do not fall between project participants."],
      [3,"3B","What is the usual progression of application testing?","Unit testing checks components; integration checks interfaces; system testing checks the full system; acceptance testing confirms business readiness."],
      [3,"3B","Who performs user acceptance testing, and what does it establish?","Business users perform UAT to determine whether the system meets business needs and is acceptable for production."],
      [3,"3B","How do quality assurance testing and UAT differ?","QA focuses on technical conformance to specifications; UAT focuses on business requirements and workflows."],
      [3,"3B","What does regression testing verify?","That a change or fix has not damaged functions that previously worked."],
      [3,"3B","What is parallel testing used for during a system change?","Run old and new systems with comparable inputs and reconcile outputs to assess whether the new system behaves as expected."],
      [3,"3B","What is the key control objective during data conversion?","Show converted data is complete and accurate by reconciling source and target records and investigating exceptions."],
      [3,"3B","What do the ACID properties protect in database transactions?","Atomicity (all or none), consistency (valid state), isolation (transactions do not interfere improperly), and durability (committed changes persist)."],
      [3,"3B","When is a pilot test useful?","Deploy to a limited group or location first, observe real-world issues, and resolve them before wider rollout."],
      [3,"3B","What distinguishes white-box from black-box testing?","White-box testing examines internal logic and structure; black-box testing evaluates behavior without relying on internal code knowledge."],
      [3,"3B","Why control test data and results?","Protect sensitive information, make tests authorized and repeatable, and retain evidence that requirements and controls were tested."],
      [4,"4A","Which OSI layers are associated with IP routing and MAC-based switching?","IP routing is a Layer 3 network function; forwarding frames by MAC address is a Layer 2 data-link function."],
      [4,"4A","What is the purpose of a VLAN?","Logically segment a Layer 2 network into separate broadcast domains and limit unnecessary traffic."],
      [4,"4A","How do incident management and problem management differ?","Incident management restores service quickly; problem management addresses underlying causes to prevent recurrence."],
      [4,"4A","Why authorize, test, and track production changes?","Reduce unauthorized or harmful changes, preserve system integrity, and maintain traceability from request through review."],
      [4,"4A","What should an auditor verify about batch job scheduling?","Jobs are authorized and sequenced correctly; failures are logged and followed up; privileged scheduling changes are controlled."],
      [4,"4A","What does capacity management aim to balance?","Current and forecast demand against resources and agreed performance levels, with timely action on bottlenecks."],
      [4,"4A","Why reconcile data passed between interfacing systems?","Detect missing, duplicated, or altered transactions and establish transfers are complete and accurate."],
      [4,"4A","What is the auditor's focus when reviewing database access?","Whether access is authorized and least-privileged, privileged activity is monitored, and conflicting duties are separated."],
      [4,"4A","What should an IT service-level measure represent?","A measurable commitment tied to customer needs, with clear measurement, reporting, ownership, and escalation."],
      [4,"4A","Why maintain IT asset inventories?","Track ownership, location, lifecycle, licensing, and exposure to operational or security risk."],
      [4,"4A","A batch job fails overnight and reports are incomplete. What control should the auditor expect?","Failure alerts, documented restart procedures, and evidence an authorized operator investigates and resolves the exception."],
      [4,"4A","An emergency production change bypasses normal approval. What should happen afterward?","Document the justification, obtain required retrospective review, test as feasible, and update configuration records."],
      [4,"4A","Why separate development, test, and production environments?","Reduce the chance that unapproved code or test activity affects live data and services; control access and promotion between environments."],
      [4,"4A","A service meets its uptime target but users report slow response. What should performance management examine?","End-to-end response times, workload and capacity trends, and user-facing performance, not availability alone."],
      [4,"4A","What should happen when the same service incident recurs?","Restore service for each incident, then use problem management to identify root cause and track lasting corrective action."],
      [4,"4A","Why tightly control administrator access to production databases?","Privileged access may bypass application controls; restrict and log it, review activity independently, and separate conflicting duties."],
      [4,"4A","What is a key risk of uncontrolled end-user spreadsheets?","Critical decisions may depend on untested formulas or undocumented changes; identify, validate, and govern important spreadsheets."],
      [4,"4A","An interface reports success, but record totals differ. What controls should the auditor look for?","Independent counts or control totals, acknowledgments, and investigation of rejected or unmatched items to establish completeness and accuracy."],
      [4,"4A","Why monitor both scheduled maintenance and security patches?","Keep systems supported and address vulnerabilities without introducing untested changes or disrupting critical services."],
      [4,"4B","What questions should a business impact analysis answer?","Which processes are critical, which resources support them, and how long disruption is tolerable before impact becomes unacceptable."],
      [4,"4B","How do RTO and RPO differ?","RTO is the target time to restore service. RPO is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time."],
      [4,"4B","What does maximum tolerable downtime represent?","The longest a process can be unavailable before impact is unacceptable; recovery objectives should fit within that limit."],
      [4,"4B","How do a BCP and a DRP differ?","A BCP addresses continuation of critical business operations. A DRP focuses on restoring IT services and infrastructure."],
      [4,"4B","Who is the authoritative source for process criticality in a BIA?","The business process owner, who understands operational, financial, legal, and customer consequences of disruption."],
      [4,"4B","What is the trade-off when choosing recovery objectives?","Shorter recovery times and lower data loss usually require more resilience investment; the BIA balances cost against disruption impact."],
      [4,"4B","How do hot, warm, and cold recovery sites generally differ?","A hot site is ready for rapid use; a warm site needs some setup; a cold site offers space but requires the most preparation."],
      [4,"4B","Why must backup restoration be tested?","A successful backup job does not prove data can be restored. Test restores verify recoverability, integrity, and recovery objectives."],
      [4,"4B","What makes a continuity exercise valuable?","It tests people, communications, dependencies, and procedures against scenarios, then tracks identified gaps."],
      [4,"4B","Why update continuity plans after organizational changes?","Changes to processes, systems, suppliers, locations, or roles can invalidate contacts, dependencies, assumptions, and recovery steps."],
      [4,"4B","A recovery test meets its RTO but restores data older than the RPO. Did recovery succeed?","No. The time target was met, but the data-loss objective was not; investigate backup frequency, replication, and recovery procedures."],
      [4,"4B","What should drive BIA recovery priorities?","Business-process impact and dependencies, informed by process owners, rather than IT convenience or replacement cost alone."],
      [4,"4B","Why store backup copies separately from the production environment?","Reduce the chance that one site event, outage, or compromise destroys both production data and its recoverable copies."],
      [4,"4B","What should an auditor verify about off-site backup storage?","Access protection, environmental safeguards, inventory and transport controls, retention, and timely retrieval and restoration capability."],
      [4,"4B","A plan lists old contacts and a retired recovery site. What does that indicate?","Plan maintenance is ineffective; validate contacts, resources, dependencies, and recovery arrangements through periodic review and exercises."],
      [4,"4B","What does redundancy improve, and what does it not replace?","Redundancy can reduce single points of failure, but does not replace tested backups, recovery procedures, or protection from shared-site failures."],
      [4,"4B","What should a disaster recovery test demonstrate?","That assigned personnel can restore prioritized services and data within approved recovery objectives."],
      [4,"4B","Why include suppliers and business users in continuity exercises?","Critical dependencies cross organizational boundaries; participation tests coordination and exposes missing assumptions."],
      [4,"4B","Who should approve recovery priorities and objectives?","Business leadership and process owners set priorities based on impact; IT advises on feasibility and recovery capabilities."],
      [5,"5A","How do a security policy, standard, guideline, and procedure differ?","A policy states intent; a standard sets mandatory requirements; a guideline recommends practices; a procedure gives step-by-step instructions."],
      [5,"5A","What do confidentiality, integrity, and availability protect?","Confidentiality limits disclosure; integrity protects accuracy and completeness; availability ensures authorized access when needed."],
      [5,"5A","What principle should guide user access permissions?","Least privilege: grant only access needed for assigned duties, for only as long as needed, and review it when roles change."],
      [5,"5A","How do symmetric and asymmetric encryption differ?","Symmetric encryption uses a shared secret key. Asymmetric cryptography uses a related public/private key pair."],
      [5,"5A","What does a cryptographic hash provide, and not provide by itself?","A hash helps detect changes using a fixed-length digest; by itself it does not encrypt data or prove who created it."],
      [5,"5A","What role does a certificate authority perform in PKI?","It validates identities according to its process and issues signed certificates that bind identities to public keys."],
      [5,"5A","Why segment networks and separate sensitive systems?","Limit unnecessary connectivity and contain the spread or impact of compromise while enforcing distinct access rules."],
      [5,"5A","What should a data classification scheme drive?","Handling, access, retention, labeling, and protection requirements proportionate to sensitivity and business impact."],
      [5,"5A","What is the purpose of a secure configuration baseline?","Define a consistent minimum configuration, verify deployed assets conform, and manage approved exceptions."],
      [5,"5A","What should an auditor consider when evaluating privacy controls?","Clear responsibilities and appropriate practices for collecting, using, accessing, retaining, disclosing, and disposing of personal information."],
      [5,"5A","A former employee's account remains active. What is the risk and expected control?","Unauthorized access may continue; promptly disable accounts and revoke credentials, tokens, and physical access through coordinated offboarding."],
      [5,"5A","What does multifactor authentication require?","Two or more distinct factor types, such as something known, possessed, or inherent; two passwords alone are not two factors."],
      [5,"5A","What assurance does a digital signature support, and what does it not provide by itself?","It supports origin authentication and integrity; it does not by itself provide confidentiality."],
      [5,"5A","Why encrypt sensitive data both at rest and in transit?","Reduce exposure if storage or communications are accessed without authorization; key management remains essential."],
      [5,"5A","What should an auditor verify about encryption keys?","Keys are securely generated and stored, access-restricted, retired or rotated appropriately, and recoverable through controlled procedures."],
      [5,"5A","Why review physical access to a server room?","Ensure entry is authorized, logged, monitored, and promptly removed when no longer needed, reducing tampering and disruption risk."],
      [5,"5A","What is the purpose of data loss prevention controls?","Detect or restrict unauthorized movement of sensitive information, with alerts reviewed and exceptions handled."],
      [5,"5A","What risk does an unmanaged wireless access point create?","It may bypass approved network security; detect unauthorized access points and enforce secure configuration and authentication."],
      [5,"5A","Why apply secure settings to mobile and IoT devices?","They may store sensitive data and connect to networks; use approved configurations, updates, access controls, and remote-loss protections."],
      [5,"5B","How do awareness, training, and education differ?","Awareness builds recognition and behavior; training develops task-specific skills; education provides deeper knowledge and understanding."],
      [5,"5B","How can an organization evaluate whether security awareness is working?","Use outcomes such as incident trends, reporting behavior, and phishing-simulation results, then improve the program."],
      [5,"5B","How do a vulnerability scan and a penetration test differ?","A scan identifies potential weaknesses. A penetration test attempts controlled exploitation to demonstrate likely impact and attack paths."],
      [5,"5B","How does an IDS differ from an IPS?","An IDS detects and alerts on suspicious activity. An IPS can detect and block activity, so rules and service impact need careful management."],
      [5,"5B","What is the first priority when responding to a security incident?","Follow the response process to protect people and limit harm, while preserving evidence and escalating through designated channels."],
      [5,"5B","Why preserve chain of custody for digital evidence?","Document who collected, handled, transferred, and stored evidence so integrity and provenance can be supported."],
      [5,"5B","Why synchronize clocks and protect logs?","Consistent timestamps help correlate events; access and retention safeguards help preserve logs as reliable evidence."],
      [5,"5B","What should an incident response plan define?","Roles, escalation criteria, communication, containment and recovery, evidence handling, and post-incident review."],
      [5,"5B","What is the purpose of post-incident lessons learned?","Identify causes and response gaps, update controls and plans, and track actions to reduce recurrence."],
      [5,"5B","What should guide the priority of a vulnerability fix?","Risk, exploitability, asset criticality, exposure, and business impact within a defined remediation and exception process."],
      [5,"5B","A scan reports vulnerabilities on an internet-facing critical server. How should remediation be prioritized?","Validate findings and prioritize by exploitability, exposure, asset criticality, and business impact; track exceptions and verify remediation."],
      [5,"5B","Why avoid altering original digital evidence during an investigation?","Changes can destroy metadata or undermine integrity; preserve originals, work from verified copies, and document handling."],
      [5,"5B","A malware alert is confirmed on one workstation. What response objective comes before restoring normal service?","Contain the threat to limit spread, preserve necessary evidence, and follow the approved response and escalation process."],
      [5,"5B","What is the difference between a false positive and a false negative in monitoring?","A false positive flags benign activity; a false negative fails to identify actual malicious activity. Both affect detection quality."],
      [5,"5B","Why correlate logs from endpoints, network devices, and identity systems?","Combined events can show attack paths and scope that may not be visible in a single log source."],
      [5,"5B","What should happen after a penetration test?","Prioritize and remediate validated findings, assign owners and deadlines, then retest; testing alone does not reduce risk."],
      [5,"5B","Why coordinate security-incident communications?","Accurate, authorized communication limits confusion, protects sensitive details, and meets applicable obligations."],
      [5,"5B","What determines whether an incident is a reportable privacy breach?","Applicable law and policy; assess affected data and people with designated privacy and legal roles against notification criteria."],
      [5,"5B","Why test security-alert escalation paths?","An alert is ineffective if no responsible person receives, acknowledges, and acts on it within the required timeframe."],
      [1,"1A","What do ISACA audit standards, guidelines, and the Code of Professional Ethics contribute?","Standards set required professional expectations, guidelines support consistent practice, and the Code establishes ethical duties such as objectivity, confidentiality, and due care."],
